									  What
									 ======
A libary that allows you to ergonomically unmarshal csv's into structs
```go
package example

type Content struct {
	Betrag float64 `csv:"A"`
	Text string `csv:"b"`
	Enum int `csv:"c"`
}

func main() {
	f, _ := os.Open(filename)
	defer f.Close()
	r := csv_go.NewReader[Content](f)
	//r.SetComma(';') // if you have european csv's
	//r.SetGermanDecimal(true) // if you have floats as 1,35
	//_ = r.SkipLine() // if you have a column name header to skip

	for line := range r.Iter() {
		// DO WHATEVER
	}
	// There is also a .Read if you hate iterators
}
```
Supported types you can have in you struct: int, string, float32, float64.
You can have the column letters in upper or lower case
multi-letter columnnames also work (eg. "AC", "aac")

									  Why
									 =====
Have been doing alot of csv transformation scripts and having to write the
serializing/deserializing to and from string slices to use the nice csv libary
sucked. Had a few functions already written but wasn't as refined as I would
have liked so this is me polishing it to my liking and packaging it for future
use.
